    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Dynamic or reversible polymer networks (DPNs) can enable reprocessable polymers for a circular economy and a sustainable future. The mechanical properties of these networks change based on tunable chemical parameters, such as the crosslinking probability, chain length, and backbone stiffness. Revealing the power law scaling of these mechanical proper ties with respect to the crosslinking probability near the gel point, a critical transition from solution to gel during chemical processing, is crucial for designing reprocessable polymers. In particular, the power law scaling of the tracer diffusivity, which directly contributes to the strength and flexibility of the network, is unknown. \r\nHere, we perform hybrid Monte Carlo-molecular dynamics simulations to model reversible networks near the gel point. We showed that previous assumptions of a constant diffusivity with respect to crosslinking probability are incorrect. Our results suggest that tracer diffusivity decreases as the network becomes more gel-like, and that there is likely a universal function for tracer diffusivity in reversible polymer networks. This universal function of diffusivity is essential to deriving a coherent theory for power law scaling of dynamic properties in DPNs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Yang, X. (2024). Scaling of Tracer Diffusivity in Dynamic Polymer Networks. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/bk313pt9859. https://doi.org/10.25740/bk313pt9859.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
